DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
   SSttaannddaarrdd ddeessccrriippttiioonn
       (From POSIX.1) The vvffoorrkk() function has the same effect as ffoorrkk(2), ex‐
       cept  that  the behavior is undefined if the process created by vvffoorrkk()
       either modifies any data other than a variable of type  _p_i_d___t  used  to
       store  the  return  value from vvffoorrkk(), or returns from the function in
       which vvffoorrkk() was called, or calls any other function  before  success‐
       fully calling __eexxiitt(2) or one of the eexxeecc(3) family of functions.

   LLiinnuuxx ddeessccrriippttiioonn
       vvffoorrkk(),  just  like  ffoorrkk(2),  creates  a child process of the calling
       process.  For details and return value and errors, see ffoorrkk(2).

       vvffoorrkk() is a special case of  cclloonnee(2).   It  is  used  to  create  new
       processes  without  copying  the page tables of the parent process.  It
       may be useful in performance‐sensitive applications where  a  child  is
       created which then immediately issues an eexxeeccvvee(2).

       vvffoorrkk()  differs  from  ffoorrkk(2) in that the calling thread is suspended
       until the child terminates (either normally, by  calling  __eexxiitt(2),  or
       abnormally,  after  delivery  of a fatal signal), or it makes a call to
       eexxeeccvvee(2).  Until that point, the child shares all memory with its par‐
       ent, including the stack.  The child must not return from  the  current
       function  or  call eexxiitt(3) (which would have the effect of calling exit
       handlers established by the parent process and  flushing  the  parent’s
       ssttddiioo(3) buffers), but may call __eexxiitt(2).

       As  with  ffoorrkk(2), the child process created by vvffoorrkk() inherits copies
       of various of the caller’s process attributes (e.g., file  descriptors,
       signal  dispositions,  and current working directory); the vvffoorrkk() call
       differs only in the treatment of the  virtual  address  space,  as  de‐
       scribed above.

       Signals sent to the parent arrive after the child releases the parent’s
       memory (i.e., after the child terminates or calls eexxeeccvvee(2)).

   HHiissttoorriicc ddeessccrriippttiioonn
       Under  Linux,  ffoorrkk(2) is implemented using copy‐on‐write pages, so the
       only penalty incurred by ffoorrkk(2) is the time and memory required to du‐
       plicate the parent’s page tables, and to create a unique task structure
       for the child.  However, in the bad old days a  ffoorrkk(2)  would  require
       making  a  complete  copy of the caller’s data space, often needlessly,
       since usually immediately afterward an  eexxeecc(3)  is  done.   Thus,  for
       greater  efficiency,  BSD introduced the vvffoorrkk() system call, which did
       not fully copy the address space of the parent  process,  but  borrowed
       the  parent’s memory and thread of control until a call to eexxeeccvvee(2) or
       an exit occurred.  The parent process was suspended while the child was
       using its resources.  The use of vvffoorrkk() was tricky: for  example,  not
       modifying  data  in  the parent process depended on knowing which vari‐
       ables were held in a register.

VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       The requirements put on vvffoorrkk() by the standards are weaker than  those
       put  on  ffoorrkk(2),  so an implementation where the two are synonymous is
       compliant.  In particular, the programmer cannot rely on the parent re‐
       maining blocked until the child either terminates or  calls  eexxeeccvvee(2),
       and cannot rely on any specific behavior with respect to shared memory.

       Some  consider the semantics of vvffoorrkk() to be an architectural blemish,
       and the 4.2BSD man page stated: “This system call  will  be  eliminated
       when  proper  system  sharing mechanisms are implemented.  Users should
       not depend on the memory sharing semantics of _v_f_o_r_k as it will, in that
       case, be made synonymous to _f_o_r_k.”  However, even though modern  memory
       management  hardware  has  decreased the performance difference between
       ffoorrkk(2) and vvffoorrkk(), there are various reasons why Linux and other sys‐
       tems have retained vvffoorrkk():

       •  Some performance‐critical applications require the small performance
          advantage conferred by vvffoorrkk().

       •  vvffoorrkk() can be implemented on systems that lack a  memory‐management
          unit  (MMU),  but  ffoorrkk(2)  can’t  be  implemented  on such systems.
          (POSIX.1‐2008 removed vvffoorrkk() from the standard; the POSIX rationale
          for the ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn(3) function notes that that function, which pro‐
          vides functionality equivalent to ffoorrkk(2)+eexxeecc(3), is designed to be
          implementable on systems that lack an MMU.)

       •  On systems where memory is constrained, vvffoorrkk() avoids the  need  to
          temporarily commit memory (see the description of _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_y_s_/_v_m_/_o_v_e_r_‐
          _c_o_m_m_i_t___m_e_m_o_r_y  in pprroocc(5)) in order to execute a new program.  (This
          can be especially beneficial where a large parent process wishes  to
          execute  a  small  helper program in a child process.)  By contrast,
          using ffoorrkk(2) in this scenario requires either committing an  amount
          of  memory  equal to the size of the parent process (if strict over‐
          committing is in force) or overcommitting memory with the risk  that
          a process is terminated by the out‐of‐memory (OOM) killer.

   LLiinnuuxx nnootteess
       Fork handlers established using pptthhrreeaadd__aattffoorrkk(3) are not called when a
       multithreaded  program  employing  the  NPTL  threading  library  calls
       vvffoorrkk().  Fork handlers are called in this case in a program using  the
       LinuxThreads  threading library.  (See pptthhrreeaaddss(7) for a description of
       Linux threading libraries.)

       A call to vvffoorrkk() is equivalent to calling cclloonnee(2) with  _f_l_a_g_s  speci‐
       fied as:

